    "evidence_text": "CNN .\n\nAll experiments were run on a single machine consisting of a 3.4 GHz Intel Core i7 CPU with 16 GB of RAM and an NVidia GeForce Titan Xp GPU.\n\nResults and ablations ::: Ablation studies\tTo understand the impact of some hyperparameters on performance, we conducted additional experiments on the Reuters, Polarity, and IMDB datasets, with the non-hierarchical version of MPAD.\n\nNumber of MP iterations.\n\nsee in Table TABREF29 that having more iterations improves performance.\n\nIndeed, in initial experiments involving readout at $t$=$T$ only, setting $T\\ge 2$ was always decreasing performance, despite the GRU-based updates (Eq. DISPLAY_FORM14).\n\nto better performance, while on Polarity and IMDB, it is the opposite.\n\nRemoving the master node deteriorates performance across all datasets, clearly showing the value of having such a node.\n\nAs shown in Table TABREF29, using sum instead of mean decreases performance everywhere, suggesting that in our application, capturing the distribution of neighbor representations is more important that capturing their structure.\n\nExcept on Reuters (almost no change), performance always suffers, stressing the need to take into account the root node during updates, not only its neighborhood.",
